1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:04,219
Ten hard SAT Reading
and Writing questions.

2
00:00:04,239 --> 00:00:10,140
All of them original, all of them built
to feel like the tougher questions on

3
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:11,420
the real test.

4
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:14,500
If you're taking the SAT on December 5,

5
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:17,050
this is a good check on where you stand.

6
00:00:17,570 --> 00:00:19,150
Same rules as always.

7
00:00:19,170 --> 00:00:21,429
Pause before every
answer, try it yourself,

8
00:00:21,449 --> 00:00:22,630
and keep score.

9
00:00:22,650 --> 00:00:26,029
They get harder as we go
and cover all four domains.

10
00:00:26,049 --> 00:00:30,100
At the end, I'll show you what your
misses say about what to study.

11
00:00:30,531 --> 00:00:31,511
Question one.

12
00:00:31,531 --> 00:00:34,591
Because the fossil
consists of a single tooth,

13
00:00:34,611 --> 00:00:39,761
the paleontologists who described it have
called their classification blank they

14
00:00:39,781 --> 00:00:44,641
assigned the tooth to a new species of
early crocodile relative but noted that

15
00:00:44,661 --> 00:00:48,791
the assignment could change if
more complete remains are found.

16
00:00:49,223 --> 00:00:50,633
Pause and try it.

17
00:00:57,407 --> 00:01:02,377
The colon after the blank tells
you the second half explains it.

18
00:01:02,447 --> 00:01:05,107
The tooth goes in a new species for now,

19
00:01:05,127 --> 00:01:06,267
but that could change.

20
00:01:06,287 --> 00:01:09,267
So predict something like temporary.

21
00:01:09,287 --> 00:01:10,466
That's provisional.

22
00:01:10,486 --> 00:01:12,017
The answer is A.

23
00:01:12,485 --> 00:01:16,014
The trap is C, contentious,
which means disputed.

24
00:01:16,244 --> 00:01:18,665
But nobody in the text is arguing.

25
00:01:18,685 --> 00:01:22,185
The scientists are just being
careful about their own work.

26
00:01:22,205 --> 00:01:26,255
Definitive is the opposite,
and redundant doesn't fit.

27
00:01:26,778 --> 00:01:28,038
Question two.

28
00:01:28,058 --> 00:01:34,757
An exhibition brought together works by
three twentieth-century Mexican artists:

29
00:01:34,777 --> 00:01:40,618
Frida Kahlo, known for her
vivid blank Diego Rivera,

30
00:01:40,638 --> 00:01:45,618
whose murals depict scenes from
Mexican history and working life;

31
00:01:45,638 --> 00:01:51,058
and Rufino Tamayo, who blended
European modernist styles with Mexico's

32
00:01:51,078 --> 00:01:53,088
pre-Columbian traditions.

33
00:01:53,579 --> 00:01:54,989
Pause and try it.

34
00:02:01,763 --> 00:02:03,173
Look past the blank.

35
00:02:03,643 --> 00:02:06,813
Before Rufino Tamayo, there's a semicolon.

36
00:02:06,963 --> 00:02:10,222
When list items already
have commas inside them,

37
00:02:10,242 --> 00:02:14,902
semicolons separate the items,
and the separators have to match.

38
00:02:14,922 --> 00:02:17,703
So the blank needs a semicolon too.

39
00:02:17,723 --> 00:02:19,453
The answer is B.

40
00:02:19,863 --> 00:02:21,802
A comma is the trap.

41
00:02:21,822 --> 00:02:27,403
It's normal in a list, but here it blurs
where Kahlo's description ends and the

42
00:02:27,423 --> 00:02:29,393
next artist begins.

43
00:02:29,663 --> 00:02:35,153
A colon can't separate list items,
and no punctuation runs them together.

44
00:02:35,552 --> 00:02:39,052
Question three has a graph, and the
numbers are made up for practice.

45
00:02:39,072 --> 00:02:42,732
A team of plant biologists
grew two species of grass,

46
00:02:42,752 --> 00:02:47,812
species one and species two, with
and without a nitrogen fertilizer,

47
00:02:47,832 --> 00:02:51,441
and measured the plants' average
height after eight weeks.

48
00:02:51,950 --> 00:02:56,130
The team concluded that adding fertilizer
had a greater effect on the growth of

49
00:02:56,150 --> 00:03:00,489
species one than on the
growth of species two.

50
00:03:00,509 --> 00:03:05,489
Which choice most effectively uses data
from the graph to support the team's

51
00:03:05,509 --> 00:03:06,560
conclusion?

52
00:03:07,038 --> 00:03:08,448
Pause and try it.

53
00:03:16,222 --> 00:03:20,402
The claim is about effect, which
means the change from no fertilizer to

54
00:03:20,422 --> 00:03:21,562
fertilizer.

55
00:03:21,582 --> 00:03:25,082
Species one goes from thirty
to forty-two centimeters,

56
00:03:25,102 --> 00:03:26,551
a gain of twelve.

57
00:03:26,861 --> 00:03:29,882
Species two goes from forty-five to fifty,

58
00:03:29,902 --> 00:03:31,361
a gain of five.

59
00:03:31,381 --> 00:03:33,002
Twelve beats five.

60
00:03:33,022 --> 00:03:34,632
The answer is D.

61
00:03:35,106 --> 00:03:36,926
A and B are the traps.

62
00:03:36,946 --> 00:03:40,166
They point at the tallest
bars, but that's height,

63
00:03:40,186 --> 00:03:42,686
not how much the fertilizer changed it.

64
00:03:42,706 --> 00:03:47,396
And C is true for both species, so
it can't show a bigger effect on one.

65
00:03:47,838 --> 00:03:49,128
Question four.

66
00:03:49,318 --> 00:03:53,328
Although many people picture
deserts as nearly lifeless,

67
00:03:53,558 --> 00:03:58,538
the Sonoran Desert, which spans parts
of the southwestern United States and

68
00:03:58,558 --> 00:04:04,608
northwestern Mexico, is the most
biologically diverse desert in North America.

69
00:04:04,628 --> 00:04:08,768
Blank it is home to more than two
thousand native plant species,

70
00:04:08,788 --> 00:04:14,317
including the saguaro cactus, which
grows in the wild nowhere else on Earth.

71
00:04:14,836 --> 00:04:16,246
Pause and try it.

72
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:26,600
The word although pulls
you toward a contrast,

73
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:30,320
but that contrast is finished
inside the first sentence.

74
00:04:30,340 --> 00:04:33,390
Sentence one: it's the
most diverse desert.

75
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:38,230
Sentence two: here's the evidence,
two thousand plant species.

76
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:40,750
The second backs up the first.

77
00:04:41,207 --> 00:04:45,347
A transition that backs up a
claim with evidence is in fact.

78
00:04:45,367 --> 00:04:47,257
The answer is C.

79
00:04:47,367 --> 00:04:51,027
Still would mean the second
sentence is true despite the first.

80
00:04:51,047 --> 00:04:56,137
Similarly needs a similar case, and
meanwhile means at the same time.

81
00:04:56,594 --> 00:04:58,004
Question five.

82
00:04:58,234 --> 00:05:03,294
Violins made by Antonio Stradivari
about three hundred years ago are widely

83
00:05:03,314 --> 00:05:07,524
believed to have a tone that
no modern instrument can match.

84
00:05:07,584 --> 00:05:13,244
In two studies, however, experienced
violinists played old Italian violins,

85
00:05:13,264 --> 00:05:18,244
including several by Stradivari, alongside
high-quality new ones without being

86
00:05:18,264 --> 00:05:20,913
able to see which instrument they held.

87
00:05:21,382 --> 00:05:24,842
The players could not
reliably tell old from new,

88
00:05:24,862 --> 00:05:28,112
and most preferred a new violin.

89
00:05:28,262 --> 00:05:32,802
Such results do not show that
Stradivari's instruments are ordinary,

90
00:05:32,822 --> 00:05:38,282
but they suggest that the violins'
reputation may rest partly on their history

91
00:05:38,302 --> 00:05:40,752
rather than on their sound alone.

92
00:05:40,772 --> 00:05:44,861
Which choice best describes the
overall structure of the text?

93
00:05:45,306 --> 00:05:46,716
Pause and try it.

94
00:05:54,491 --> 00:05:55,191
Map it.

95
00:05:55,211 --> 00:05:57,980
Sentence one: a widely held belief.

96
00:05:58,171 --> 00:06:00,621
Then research that challenges it.

97
00:06:00,811 --> 00:06:06,470
Then a careful last sentence: the results
don't show the violins are ordinary.

98
00:06:06,490 --> 00:06:09,701
They only suggest something
about the reputation.

99
00:06:10,228 --> 00:06:11,248
So the answer is B.

100
00:06:11,268 --> 00:06:12,368
C is the trap.

101
00:06:12,388 --> 00:06:17,808
It matches the first two parts, but says
the text calls the belief disproved,

102
00:06:17,828 --> 00:06:20,717
and the last sentence says the opposite.

103
00:06:21,108 --> 00:06:23,968
D says the research supports the belief,

104
00:06:23,988 --> 00:06:26,358
and A isn't in the text.

105
00:06:26,796 --> 00:06:28,206
Question six.

106
00:06:28,316 --> 00:06:33,456
The Panama Canal, which opened in
1914 and which was expanded with

107
00:06:33,476 --> 00:06:40,306
new, larger locks in 2016, blank
ships to travel between the Atlantic

108
00:06:40,326 --> 00:06:46,295
and Pacific Oceans without sailing
around the southern tip of South America.

109
00:06:46,750 --> 00:06:48,160
Pause and try it.

110
00:06:54,934 --> 00:06:57,864
Strip out the extra
information between the commas.

111
00:06:57,974 --> 00:07:03,153
Everything from which opened to
2016 is one long description.

112
00:07:03,173 --> 00:07:06,034
What's left: the Panama Canal, blank,

113
00:07:06,054 --> 00:07:08,714
ships to travel between the oceans.

114
00:07:08,734 --> 00:07:11,264
The sentence still needs its main verb.

115
00:07:11,782 --> 00:07:15,561
Allowing and having allowed
can't be a main verb.

116
00:07:15,581 --> 00:07:18,882
That allows turns the rest
into another description,

117
00:07:18,902 --> 00:07:22,642
so the sentence still never
says what the canal does.

118
00:07:22,662 --> 00:07:25,682
Only allows gives it a real main verb.

119
00:07:25,702 --> 00:07:27,552
The answer is D.

120
00:07:28,041 --> 00:07:29,331
Question seven.

121
00:07:29,681 --> 00:07:34,621
Hope is the thing with feathers
is an 1891 poem by Emily

122
00:07:34,641 --> 00:07:35,581
Dickinson.

123
00:07:35,601 --> 00:07:40,781
In the poem, the speaker suggests that
hope requires nothing from the person who

124
00:07:40,801 --> 00:07:41,801
has it.

125
00:07:41,961 --> 00:07:46,411
Which quotation from the poem most
effectively illustrates the claim?

126
00:07:46,866 --> 00:07:47,876
The choices.

127
00:07:48,226 --> 00:07:53,716
A: Hope is the thing with
feathers, that perches in the soul.

128
00:07:53,866 --> 00:07:57,366
B: And sweetest in the gale is heard,

129
00:07:57,386 --> 00:07:59,766
and sore must be the storm.

130
00:07:59,786 --> 00:08:01,886
C: I've heard it in the chillest land,

131
00:08:01,906 --> 00:08:03,526
and on the strangest sea.

132
00:08:03,546 --> 00:08:08,996
D: Yet, never, in extremity,
it asked a crumb of me.

133
00:08:09,453 --> 00:08:10,863
Pause and try it.

134
00:08:18,637 --> 00:08:24,167
Hold on to the exact claim: hope
requires nothing from the person.

135
00:08:24,477 --> 00:08:28,096
D says that even in
extremity, the worst moments,

136
00:08:28,116 --> 00:08:31,377
it never asked even a
crumb of the speaker.

137
00:08:31,397 --> 00:08:33,577
That's requiring nothing.

138
00:08:33,597 --> 00:08:35,487
The answer is D.

139
00:08:35,957 --> 00:08:38,176
B and C are the traps.

140
00:08:38,196 --> 00:08:42,977
They're about hope in hard places,
the storm and the coldest land.

141
00:08:42,997 --> 00:08:48,446
That fits the mood, but neither says
anything about what hope asks for.

142
00:08:48,877 --> 00:08:51,766
A just introduces hope as a bird.

143
00:08:52,231 --> 00:08:53,481
Question eight.

144
00:08:53,671 --> 00:08:55,240
A student's notes.

145
00:08:55,431 --> 00:09:00,290
Prussian blue is a synthetic pigment
first made in Berlin around seventeen oh

146
00:09:00,310 --> 00:09:01,321
six.

147
00:09:01,390 --> 00:09:05,921
Large quantities of it began
arriving in Japan in 1829.

148
00:09:06,081 --> 00:09:11,381
Before then, Japanese woodblock printmakers
had relied mainly on blues made from

149
00:09:11,401 --> 00:09:14,290
plants, such as indigo and dayflower.

150
00:09:14,729 --> 00:09:19,509
Prussian blue is more vivid and more
resistant to fading than those plant-based

151
00:09:19,529 --> 00:09:20,509
blues.

152
00:09:20,529 --> 00:09:25,868
And Katsushika Hokusai used it in his
woodblock print The Great Wave off

153
00:09:25,888 --> 00:09:28,629
Kanagawa, from around 1831.

154
00:09:28,649 --> 00:09:33,109
The student wants to explain an advantage
of the pigment Hokusai used in The

155
00:09:33,129 --> 00:09:35,109
Great Wave off Kanagawa.

156
00:09:35,129 --> 00:09:40,269
Which choice most effectively uses
relevant information from the notes to

157
00:09:40,289 --> 00:09:41,699
accomplish this goal?

158
00:09:42,144 --> 00:09:43,554
Pause and try it.

159
00:09:52,328 --> 00:09:56,107
The goal has two parts: name
the pigment Hokusai used,

160
00:09:56,127 --> 00:09:57,508
and give an advantage.

161
00:09:57,528 --> 00:10:03,228
Only one note states an advantage: more
vivid and more resistant to fading.

162
00:10:03,248 --> 00:10:05,588
C is the only choice that uses it.

163
00:10:05,608 --> 00:10:07,298
The answer is C.

164
00:10:07,747 --> 00:10:09,607
Every wrong choice is accurate.

165
00:10:09,627 --> 00:10:11,356
A is about timing.

166
00:10:11,427 --> 00:10:14,567
B is about where the
pigment was first made.

167
00:10:14,587 --> 00:10:16,167
D is the sneaky one.

168
00:10:16,187 --> 00:10:20,726
It describes the older plant blues,
but never says what Prussian blue does

169
00:10:20,746 --> 00:10:21,746
better.

170
00:10:22,146 --> 00:10:24,357
Background isn't an advantage.

171
00:10:24,827 --> 00:10:27,086
Question nine has two texts.

172
00:10:27,106 --> 00:10:28,126
Text one.

173
00:10:28,146 --> 00:10:33,647
In 2018, a team of researchers
used uranium-thorium dating on thin

174
00:10:33,667 --> 00:10:38,827
mineral crusts that had formed over
paintings in three Spanish caves.

175
00:10:38,847 --> 00:10:41,827
Because a crust must be younger
than the paint beneath it,

176
00:10:41,847 --> 00:10:46,497
the crusts' ages give minimum
ages for the paintings.

177
00:10:46,939 --> 00:10:51,479
Some of the paintings turned out to be
more than sixty-four thousand years old,

178
00:10:51,499 --> 00:10:55,428
long before modern humans are
thought to have arrived in Europe.

179
00:10:55,819 --> 00:10:59,229
The team concluded that
Neanderthals made the paintings.

180
00:10:59,735 --> 00:11:01,145
Text two.

181
00:11:01,535 --> 00:11:04,715
Some archaeologists remain unconvinced.

182
00:11:04,735 --> 00:11:09,515
Water flowing over cave walls can
wash uranium out of mineral crusts,

183
00:11:09,535 --> 00:11:15,395
they note, and when that happens,
uranium-thorium dating makes a crust appear

184
00:11:15,415 --> 00:11:17,705
older than it really is.

185
00:11:17,775 --> 00:11:21,115
Until the ages are
confirmed by other methods,

186
00:11:21,135 --> 00:11:26,035
these archaeologists argue, the claim
that Neanderthals made the paintings

187
00:11:26,055 --> 00:11:28,465
should be treated as unproven.

188
00:11:28,985 --> 00:11:34,885
Based on the texts, how would the
archaeologists in Text two most likely respond

189
00:11:34,905 --> 00:11:37,595
to the team's conclusion in Text one?

190
00:11:38,122 --> 00:11:39,532
Pause and try it.

191
00:11:48,306 --> 00:11:49,796
One line each.

192
00:11:49,826 --> 00:11:53,916
Text one: the dates show
Neanderthals made the art.

193
00:11:54,026 --> 00:11:56,566
Text two: the dates might be too old,

194
00:11:56,586 --> 00:11:58,035
so that's unproven.

195
00:11:58,066 --> 00:12:03,476
The answer is A: the conclusion may
rely on age estimates that are too old.

196
00:12:04,238 --> 00:12:05,858
B flips the direction.

197
00:12:05,878 --> 00:12:09,218
If uranium washes out,
the crust looks older,

198
00:12:09,238 --> 00:12:12,008
so the paintings could
be younger, not older.

199
00:12:12,317 --> 00:12:16,537
C attacks a step Text two never questions.

200
00:12:16,557 --> 00:12:20,978
Text two doubts these dates,
not the idea of dating crusts.

201
00:12:20,998 --> 00:12:22,978
And D is too extreme.

202
00:12:22,998 --> 00:12:25,128
Unproven isn't impossible.

203
00:12:25,648 --> 00:12:26,698
Last one.

204
00:12:26,848 --> 00:12:28,018
The hardest.

205
00:12:28,407 --> 00:12:34,027
Strontium from local rocks enters the
food and water of the people living in an

206
00:12:34,047 --> 00:12:34,947
area.

207
00:12:34,967 --> 00:12:41,027
So the ratio of strontium isotopes in
a person's body reflects the geology of

208
00:12:41,047 --> 00:12:44,418
the place where that person's
food and water came from.

209
00:12:44,876 --> 00:12:48,456
Tooth enamel forms in childhood
and does not change afterward,

210
00:12:48,476 --> 00:12:55,095
while bone is constantly rebuilt, so the
ratio in bone reflects roughly the last

211
00:12:55,115 --> 00:12:58,406
seven to ten years of a person's life.

212
00:12:58,926 --> 00:13:05,266
Archaeologists studying a person buried
at an ancient site found that the ratio

213
00:13:05,286 --> 00:13:10,826
in the person's tooth enamel did not
match the geology of the area around the

214
00:13:10,846 --> 00:13:15,506
site, but the ratio in
the person's bone did.

215
00:13:15,526 --> 00:13:20,076
These results suggest that
the person most likely blank.

216
00:13:20,546 --> 00:13:21,406
Pause and try it.

217
00:13:21,426 --> 00:13:23,115
Take a little longer on this one.

218
00:13:31,903 --> 00:13:34,843
Turn each clue into a time of life.

219
00:13:34,863 --> 00:13:38,643
Teeth show childhood, and
they don't match the site,

220
00:13:38,663 --> 00:13:41,042
so childhood was spent somewhere else.

221
00:13:41,062 --> 00:13:44,603
Bone shows roughly the
last seven to ten years,

222
00:13:44,623 --> 00:13:46,202
and it does match.

223
00:13:46,222 --> 00:13:50,323
So the person lived near the
site for years before death.

224
00:13:50,343 --> 00:13:51,343
That's A.

225
00:13:51,748 --> 00:13:53,488
B reverses the two clues.

226
00:13:53,508 --> 00:13:57,038
C is the trap for careful readers.

227
00:13:57,108 --> 00:14:01,887
A move only a few months before death
wouldn't give the bone time to match the

228
00:14:01,907 --> 00:14:05,488
site, since bone reflects
years, not months.

229
00:14:05,508 --> 00:14:08,678
And D is ruled out by the bone match.

230
00:14:09,133 --> 00:14:09,953
That's ten.

231
00:14:09,973 --> 00:14:11,543
Count up your score.

232
00:14:11,972 --> 00:14:14,233
The score matters less
than which ones you missed,

233
00:14:14,253 --> 00:14:16,103
so let's sort them by domain.

234
00:14:16,599 --> 00:14:19,818
On the real test, the four
domains are about twenty-eight,

235
00:14:19,838 --> 00:14:23,929
twenty-six, twenty-six, and
twenty percent of the section.

236
00:14:24,359 --> 00:14:28,779
This set used three, three,
two, and two questions,

237
00:14:28,799 --> 00:14:30,209
close to that mix.

238
00:14:30,702 --> 00:14:35,522
Questions one, five, and nine
were Craft and Structure:

239
00:14:35,542 --> 00:14:39,912
word meaning, structure, and two texts.

240
00:14:40,302 --> 00:14:44,081
If you missed those, practice
predicting a word before you look,

241
00:14:44,101 --> 00:14:46,992
and summing up each text in one line.

242
00:14:47,428 --> 00:14:51,758
Questions three, seven, and
ten were Information and Ideas:

243
00:14:52,028 --> 00:14:55,558
data, a quotation, and an inference.

244
00:14:55,868 --> 00:14:59,878
A miss here usually means the
answer didn't match the exact claim,

245
00:14:59,907 --> 00:15:02,358
or the chain of logic broke somewhere.

246
00:15:02,797 --> 00:15:06,536
Questions two and six were
